  "credit": "The Elephant Sanctuary in Tennessee, 2018 Spring Appeal. Supplied exam adaptation; exam session and individual photographers are not identified on these pages. Rights remain with their holders. The appeal images retain the supplied layout and lettering; surrounding exam material is omitted.",
  "transcript": "### Page 1 · heading and Tange\n\nTHE ELEPHANT SANCTUARY IN TENNESSEE\n\n2018 SPRING APPEAL\n\nP.O. Box 393 Hohenwald, TN 38462 | 931-796-6500 | www.elephants.com\n\nTANGE\n\nCARING FOR A 9000 LB ELEPHANT TAKES A TEAM\n\nYour Support Provides Elephants\n\nSafe Haven and Care for Life\n\nAt the age of five, African elephant Tange was captured from the wild and imported to North America. She spent the next 26 years at a zoo in Georgia until they decided to retire their elephants from public life. In 2004, Tange became one of the first African elephants at The Elephant Sanctuary.\n\nLike many of The Sanctuary’s residents, Tange, now 45, is considered to be an older, aging elephant. The health of these geriatric residents is the primary focus of our Veterinarians and Caregivers. Along with room to roam and daily husbandry and veterinary care, a well-rounded, nutritious diet is vital to their well-being.\n\nIn the wild, elephants will spend 18 hours or more a day foraging for food. In captivity, an elephant’s life looks very different. Planning and maintaining a high-quality diet for an elephant that weighs 9000 pounds takes teamwork and professional oversight.\n\nAt The Sanctuary, each elephant has an individual dietary plan to meet her specific nutritional needs. Caregivers start their day at 7.00 a.m. and work into the evening, preparing meals for the elephants and then delivering them in the 2700-acre habitat. Restaurant-grade produce is delivered weekly and has to be chopped into portions. Grains are measured, supplements weighed and hay divided. Diets change seasonally as the elephants take full advantage of the diverse natural habitats.\n\n### Page 1 · diet and Billie\n\nTange’s weekly diet includes hay, Mazuri, spinach, peanut butter, fresh produce such as carrots, apples, sweet potatoes, celery, bananas, squash, broccoli, oranges, grapes, watermelon and nutritional supplements to assist with normal age-related changes.\n\nBILLIE\n\nAdditionally, Caregivers provide food in goal-oriented ways that encourage natural foraging skills, as well as sensory activities, walking longer distances and enhancing brain function. For Billie, a 56-year-old Asian elephant who has arthritis, Caregivers provide a trail of food for her to follow with a favorite treat at the end. This promotes foraging and encourages her to take longer walks to alleviate stiff joints.\n\n### Page 2 · caregiver testimony\n\n“Providing each elephant with an individual diet is a huge part of every Caregiver’s day. It is hard work, but rewarding to see an aging elephant healthy and active.”\n\n– KRISTY, LEAD CAREGIVER\n\n### Page 2 · invitation and signature\n\nPartnering with YOU is important to ensure that all of the elephants at The Sanctuary have skilled and professional Veterinary and Husbandry care, well-maintained barns and facilities, access to an expansive natural habitat and a diet of nutritious food for life. Providing Tange, Flora, Sukari, Nosey, Shirley, Tarra, Sissy, Debbie, Ronnie, Minnie and Billie with the care they need to live a healthy life takes a team that INCLUDES YOU. We hope you will join us in 2018 as we celebrate another year of health and well-being and a future of gold-standard care for all.\n\nTHANK YOU,\n\nJanice Zeitlin, CEO\n\nMINNIE · DEBBIE\n\n### Page 2 · grocery list\n\nANNUAL ELEPHANT GROCERY LIST\n\n9 TRUCKLOADS OF HAY = 5624 BALES OF HAY\n\n1500 CRATES OF FRESH FRUITS & VEGETABLES\n\n1400 LARGE BAGS OF MAZURI\n\nSUPPLEMENTS & VITAMIN E\n\n2100 POUNDS OF PEANUT BUTTER\n\nDON’T FORGET PRUNES FOR SHIRLEY!\n\n### Supplied footnotes\n\nLB: a pound, a unit of mass used in the imperial and United States customary systems of measurement.\n\nMazuri: food pellets designed specifically for elephants.\n",
